vscode: click the language name in the status bar, pick TypeScript
open: order.provider.ts.20260917

@brief
Eight times: set the filetype to `typescript`, then open three lines under
the comment with `3o` and type the note once. Save, quit, go again.

You are not typing any slashes. Once vim knows the buffer is TypeScript it
knows `//` starts a comment, and `o` on a comment line continues it.

@keys
| `:setf typescript` | set the filetype |
| `:set ft?` | check it took |

@hints
Set the filetype first. The comment markers are vim's job after that.
--
`:setf typescript` `Enter`, then `3overified by finance` `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    :setf typescript<Enter>  3overified by finance<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Forty-three keystrokes.

This is the `formatoptions` flag `o` from the earlier drill, seen from the
other side. There it was a nuisance and you took it out; here it is doing
free typing for you. Same mechanism, and whether it helps depends entirely
on whether the next line is meant to be a comment.

Both facts arrive with the filetype. Nothing else in this drill sets
anything.

@start order.provider.ts.20260917
// restored from backup after the 17 September incident
const cutoffAt = new Date('2026-09-17T00:00:00Z');

@expected order.provider.ts.20260917
